config BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS
	bool "thingino-motors"
	select BR2_THINGINO_MOTORS_TCU if !BR2_THINGINO_MOTORS_SPI
	help
	  Userspace programs to manage motor hardware

config BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS_DW9714_ONLY
	bool "DW9714 Focus Control"
	help
	  Enable support for DW9714 VCM, includes control script

config BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS_WS
	bool "WebSocket PTZ control path"
	depends on BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS
	depends on !BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS_DW9714_ONLY
	depends on BR2_TOOLCHAIN_HAS_THREADS
	default y if BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_STREAMER_TIMPS
	help
	  Serve the PTZ control path over a WebSocket listener inside
	  motors-daemon (ws://<camera>:8089/ws by default) in addition to
	  the AF_UNIX socket the "motors" CLI speaks.

	  Without this, holding a direction arrow in the WebUI fires one
	  fetch() every 90ms, and each one costs three process spawns
	  (busybox-httpd -> json-motor.cgi -> motors, then motors -j again
	  for the status echo) plus two socket connections. With it the
	  panel opens one connection, sends one command when the button
	  goes down and one stop when it comes up, and receives position
	  updates as server pushes.

	  Enabling this compiles five extra source files into the daemon
	  (sha1, sha256, the RFC 6455 framing, the token store and the
	  motors command protocol), links -lpthread, and installs
	  json-motor-token.cgi so the WebUI page can obtain the per-boot
	  token that the listener requires. It costs roughly 25KB of text.

	  The daemon's listener is additionally gated at runtime by
	  motors.ws_enabled in /etc/thingino.json, and the WebUI falls back
	  to the CGI path whenever the socket cannot be opened, so turning
	  this on is not a one-way door.

	  Defaults on when timps is the selected streamer
	  (BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_STREAMER_TIMPS) - overridable either way, so
	  a timps build that genuinely wants the plain AF_UNIX-only daemon
	  can still turn this off. Not selected outright: this symbol has
	  its own depends on chain (a base motors build, no DW9714-only
	  focus module, a threaded toolchain), and select bypasses depends
	  on instead of respecting it, the same failure class documented on
	  WS_TLS below.

	  The source tree this pulls in switches with this option, not just
	  a branch within one tree: on, thingino-motors.mk points at Lu-Fi's
	  fork (where this feature actually lives); off, it points at
	  upstream thingino/thingino-motors. Endorsed by the upstream
	  maintainer (2026-09-01) as the intended way to carry a feature
	  that has not been merged there yet without forcing every default
	  build onto a personal fork. Point
	  THINGINO_MOTORS_OVERRIDE_SRCDIR at a local checkout instead (see
	  local.mk) only if testing changes to the WS code itself before
	  they are pushed to that fork.

config BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS_WS_TLS
	bool "wss:// (TLS) on the WebSocket PTZ listener"
	depends on BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MOTORS_WS
	# depends on, NOT select: "select BR2_PACKAGE_MBEDTLS" closed a Kconfig
	# dependency cycle - this symbol's "default y if" reads the uhttpd TLS
	# choice member, that choice's own default reads BR2_PACKAGE_MBEDTLS, and
	# the select pointed back at BR2_PACKAGE_MBEDTLS. kconfig reports
	# "recursive dependency detected" and breaks the cycle by ignoring the
	# defaults, which is why this option silently failed to auto-enable on
	# regen (2026-08-29) and even destabilized
	# BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_UHTTPD_TLS_MBEDTLS itself when this symbol was
	# absent from .config. Every image that wants wss:// already carries
	# mbedTLS (uhttpd TLS, thingino-ssl, timps), so requiring it instead of
	# selecting it costs nothing here and makes both defaults reliable again.
	depends on BR2_PACKAGE_MBEDTLS
	default y if BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_UHTTPD_TLS_MBEDTLS
	help
	  Also accept TLS-wrapped WebSocket connections, on the same
	  listener and the same port.

	  Turn this on for any camera that serves its web UI over
	  https://. A browser blocks a plain ws:// connection opened from
	  an https:// page as mixed content, so without this the PTZ panel
	  silently falls back to one CGI round trip per command - it keeps
	  working, but at exactly the latency the WebSocket path exists to
	  remove. That silent fallback is why the regression is easy to
	  miss: nothing errors, the joystick just gets sluggish again.

	  ws:// and wss:// share one port. The daemon looks at the first
	  byte a client sends (0x16 begins a TLS record, 'G' begins "GET")
	  and handles each connection accordingly, so enabling this changes
	  nothing for a camera whose web UI is still plain http://.

	  No certificate is generated here, and no second certificate
	  policy is introduced. The daemon reads whatever S95timps'
	  ensure_tls_certs() already resolved at
	  /etc/ssl/certs/timps.crt, falls back to the web UI's own
	  /etc/ssl/certs/uhttpd.crt (the same file that pair usually points
	  at) for the cases S95timps has not covered by the time motors
	  starts, and serves plain ws:// only when it finds neither.

	  Sharing the certificate rather than minting one is the point: a
	  browser trusts a self-signed certificate per origin -
	  scheme+host+PORT - and iOS Safari offers no click-through at all
	  for a failed WebSocket, so a certificate of our own on :8089
	  would simply never connect. Same reasoning, and the same
	  certificate, as the comment above ensure_tls_certs() in S95timps.

	  Costs roughly 9KB of text in motors-daemon plus a dynamic link
	  against mbedTLS (already on any image with uhttpd TLS or timps),
	  and about 21KB of heap per CONNECTED TLS client - mbedTLS's
	  per-session I/O buffers, bounded by motors.ws_max_clients
	  (default 4).
